+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+The homepage now includes a two-step rewards registration form. It uses Google Identity Services in a popup to confirm email ownership without sending a code or email link. There is no password field, client-side verification flag, or simulated success path.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Requires Node.js 22.13+ and `npm install` (or `npm ci`). Copy `.env.example` to `.env`, set `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` to a Google OAuth **Web application** client ID, and set `APP_ORIGIN` to the exact website origin. Add the same origin to the client's **Authorized JavaScript origins** in Google Cloud. No Google client secret is required. Use HTTPS in production, set `NODE_ENV=production`, and keep `DATA_DIR` on persistent storage with restricted access and backups.
+
+`npm run build` checks syntax, runs isolated cryptographic verification and storage tests, and packages the application in `dist/`. It does not start a server or import the server entrypoint. When explicitly ready to serve the application, `npm start` runs the Node HTTP server; a static file host alone cannot support registration. A packaged deployment needs Node dependencies installed and the same runtime environment variables. Both source and packaged versions use the same start command.
+
+## Verification contract
+
+- `POST /api/rewards/challenge` accepts JSON `{}` from `APP_ORIGIN`. It returns a public Google client ID and random nonce, and sets an HttpOnly, SameSite registration cookie (Secure over HTTPS). Challenges expire after ten minutes and are single-use after a successful registration.
+- The browser passes the nonce to Google, then sends `POST /api/rewards/register` with `{ name, email, credential, termsAccepted: true, marketingConsent: false }`. `credential` must be a Google-signed ID token, not a Google access token.
+- The server validates Google's signature against its rotating public keys, the issuer, audience, expiry, token age, subject, optional authorized party, nonce, verified-email claim, and matching form email. Only Gmail and Google Workspace accounts with a signed hosted-domain claim qualify. Google is not authoritative for third-party emails attached to personal Google accounts, even when their `email_verified` claim is true. See [Google's verification guidance](https://developers.google.com/identity/gsi/web/guides/verify-google-id-token).
+- Success returns HTTP 201 and `{ account: { id, name, email, emailVerified: true } }` only after the account is stored. Missing configuration returns 503; invalid identity/challenge returns 401; duplicates return 409; excessive attempts return 429. Failed responses never claim that an email is verified.
+
+Accounts persist in SQLite at `DATA_DIR/rewards.sqlite`. Email and provider subject have unique constraints. Consent choices, terms version and timestamps are stored; ID tokens are not. Registration does not establish an authenticated account session or implement reward balances/redemption. Existing homepage navigation destinations remain outside this change.
+
+The challenge and request-rate maps are process-local. Deploy as one persistent Node process; multi-instance deployments need a shared challenge/rate store. The limiter uses the direct socket address, ignoring untrusted forwarding headers; configure a trusted proxy strategy and edge rate limiting when deploying behind a reverse proxy. Restarting the process invalidates pending challenges, and customers can start again.
+
+No development/preview server or browser verification was used during implementation. A real end-to-end Google sign-up requires configured credentials and an authorized origin.
